WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.790
It has often been said that dog is man’s
best friend, and obviously that isn’t just

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.839
exclusive to men.

00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:13.970
Ask any pet owner – whether they own a dog,
cat, rabbit, hamster or soul-sucking interdimensional

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:18.730
parasitic monster with too many tentacles
– and they’ll all tell you the same.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:24.110
Your pet can be your best friend, they can
give you the most unconditional, affectionate

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:28.560
form of love possible, and as long as you
treat your little buddy right, they’ll be

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.400
loyal to you for the rest of your life and
theirs.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.280
It's not uncommon for a pet to outlive their
owner, especially if they belong to someone

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:38.460
who’s elderly.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.190
And that might be a sad thing to think about
for some.

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:46.059
Sure, it’s important to remember the positives,
like how much happiness and companionship

00:00:46.059 --> 00:00:50.269
that animal would have brought an aging owner
in their last years of life.

00:00:50.269 --> 00:00:55.269
Even someone younger who dies unexpectedly
might have had their quality of life infinitely

00:00:55.269 --> 00:00:58.530
improved by owning a loving pet to make them
smile.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:03.579
But, on the other hand, there’s the negative
downside that now some pets have to live on

00:01:03.579 --> 00:01:08.729
after a tragic death, not fully understanding
where their beloved owner went.

00:01:08.729 --> 00:01:13.360
So often we hear stories of dogs that waited
for their master to come home, only for them

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.469
to never walk through the front door again.

00:01:16.469 --> 00:01:21.770
And its equally hard when things pan out the
opposite way, and an owner loses their pet.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:26.229
The one silver lining is that we, as human
beings, have a far greater understanding of

00:01:26.229 --> 00:01:28.820
death, and the grief it causes.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:33.810
We know that, eventually, despite how hard
it can be to adjust to that initial, heart-breaking

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:39.409
loss, it is possible to move on, and for things
to one day get better.

00:01:39.409 --> 00:01:44.149
But what happens when someone can’t accept
the death of their beloved animal companion?

00:01:44.149 --> 00:01:48.329
When the unconditional love of their pet is
suddenly missing from their life, how far

00:01:48.329 --> 00:01:51.689
will a person go to recapture that feeling?

00:01:51.689 --> 00:01:56.909
How long does it take for grief born out of
love to become an unhealthy fixation?

00:01:56.909 --> 00:01:59.810
And what is the true Price of Obsession?

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:08.370
Well, the answer involves SCP-589, both what
it can give… and just as easily take away.

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:12.420
For as long as she could remember – and
even further back than that – Erin and her

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.640
dog Poncho had been inseparable.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:20.530
Ever since her mom had first adopted the scrappy
little Jack Russell Terrier, Erin had fallen

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:23.930
head-over-heels in love with the little rascal.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:28.540
Loving a pet is different from loving another
person, because they just can’t help showing

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.440
how much they love you back.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:35.290
Humans, for all their good qualities, are
so nuanced, and not everyone is honest with

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:37.220
each other all of the time.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:40.000
But a pet, especially a dog?

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.900
Even though they can’t speak, a bark or
a whimper or excited wagging of the tail can

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:49.650
easily tell you how they’re feeling, and
Poncho was no exception.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:53.820
Erin and her mom – Cleo – lived alone,
it was just the two of them for quite a long

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:54.850
time.

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:58.980
And while Cleo had little problem with that,
she couldn’t help but notice how isolating

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.680
it was for her daughter.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:05.120
From a young age, Erin had always been quiet,
kept to herself at school.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:10.150
Her mom kept expecting her to make some little
friends, ask to invite them over or vice versa.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:12.780
But it didn’t seem to be happening.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:16.850
It didn’t seem to be bothering her daughter,
but Cleo was worried that it was giving her

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:21.380
the wrong idea, that being on her own was
somehow better.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:26.690
So, in an effort to give Erin at least one
source of companionship, Poncho joined the

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:27.800
family.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:32.820
Brown patches dotted over his white fur, he
was the perfect pet, an excitable and loving

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:36.230
little puppy that Erin was immediately smitten
with.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:41.040
When Cleo told her he was hers to keep, being
only seven-years-old Erin broke down crying

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:42.680
with tears of joy.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:46.860
Her mom had let her pick out a name for him,
and she’d quickly settled on ‘Poncho’.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:52.130
At first, Erin had meant to say it differently,
in order to name the pup after a friendly

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:57.180
character from her favorite animated movie,
but had mispronounced it in her excitement

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:00.400
at meeting the energetic dog for the first
time.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:05.080
The name quickly stuck though, and as the
years went on and Poncho got bigger, Erin

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:10.120
bought her four-legged best friend a little
poncho of his own to wear when it started

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:11.120
raining.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:15.680
Over the following years, Poncho became Erin’s
most constant and loving companion.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:20.430
Even as she grew up, moved through her childhood,
and found making friends a little easier with

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:25.770
every passing year, there never came a time
when she didn’t need her best friend.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:29.740
When Erin had her first breakup in high school
and came home with floods of tears in her

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:34.580
eyes, Poncho could sense she was upset, and
came wandering up to her, sitting in her lap

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:37.160
to make her feel just a little bit better.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:42.220
Then, a few years later when her mom got sick,
Erin had to take care of her, a task that

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:46.560
would have been much harder without Poncho
there to alleviate that stress and lift her

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:47.800
and Cleo’s spirits.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:53.870
And, when Cleo eventually passed, the little
white and brown dog sat quietly with his owner

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:56.440
as she said goodbye to her mom.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:01.040
Now that it was just the two of them, Erin
and Poncho were living in a tiny apartment.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:05.570
It was cramped for one person and a dog, but
Erin was just grateful to have a place to

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:08.650
live and the company of her favorite pup.

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:13.000
Besides, Poncho wasn’t a puppy anymore,
in fact, he hadn’t raced around the park

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.320
or chased a ball for quite a long time.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:19.900
With the numerous stresses of her everyday
life, least of all holding down two jobs in

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:25.460
her desperate attempts to make enough money
to pay rent, Erin had hardly noticed the signs.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:30.650
Poncho was showing his age, it had been happening
gradually in the background over the years.

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:34.600
He wouldn’t chase his ball or really move
around much, and when he did it was little

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.580
more than a lethargic plod around the apartment.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:42.550
Perhaps it was because of such a measured,
slower-paced change that Erin was unable to

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:43.790
acknowledge it.

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:48.750
She could see her old friend was getting more
tired, sleeping longer, his tail rarely wagging

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:53.320
as much as it used to, but by now that felt
like Poncho’s normal behavior.

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:58.210
Then again, given how important her dog was
to her, it’s just as likely that she didn’t

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:02.820
want to accept the truth: that, unfortunately,
nothing lasts forever.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:08.820
He was almost fifteen in human years, which
by all accounts is an impressive age for a

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.669
dog, especially one of Poncho’s size and
breed.

00:06:11.669 --> 00:06:16.130
It was on a day that Erin was out working
her morning job when it happened.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:20.660
The faithful, adoring Jack Russell Terrier,
who had spent his years being nothing but

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:25.740
loved and giving back only more love in return,
curled up on Erin’s bed.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:31.900
The apartment was still, silent, not a sound
to be heard, save for those last, tired few

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:32.900
breaths.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:36.940
Lying there, maybe the dog wondered if he’d
get to see his friend again, if she would

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:39.010
make it back from work in time.

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:44.680
He closed his little brown eyes and peacefully
drifted off for one last sleep.

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:48.889
During the break between her shifts – after
the end of the one at her first job and before

00:06:48.889 --> 00:06:52.510
starting at her second – Erin had just enough
time to get home.

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:56.781
Usually, she had just enough time to eat and
get herself ready for the changeover, then

00:06:56.781 --> 00:06:59.810
quickly check on Poncho before having to dash
back out.

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:03.560
Poking her head into the bedroom, she saw
her dog laying right there on her bed.

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:07.410
There was a stillness to him that instantly
made her stomach drop.

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:11.650
His ears didn’t move when she called his
name, he didn’t react when she stroked his

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:12.650
fur.

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:18.470
He was gone, and the moment she realized it,
Erin felt like her whole world had come crashing

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:19.470
down.

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:24.060
The loss of her oldest and closest friend
hurt almost as much as losing her mom.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:28.510
Erin always felt that the problem with funerals
wasn’t just how sad they were, or how it

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:31.110
always seemed to rain when she went to one.

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:36.500
Instead, it was more that they could never
properly sum up just how much someone truly

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:37.500
meant.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.940
Nobody could ever condense the years’ worth
of love and memories into a burial.

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:43.419
And it was worse when losing a pet.

00:07:43.419 --> 00:07:47.449
There was no procession, no wake, nobody else
there.

00:07:47.449 --> 00:07:51.710
Just her and Poncho, saying goodbye a final
time.

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:54.140
Eventually, things got to be too much.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:57.960
The heartbreak of Poncho’s death was another
struggle in a lifetime of lows that had all

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:00.220
left their lasting wounds on Erin.

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:04.740
That, coupled with the stress of trying to
carry on with a busy life, barely able to

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:10.490
keep herself afloat in either of her jobs,
had pushed her to the edge of a breakdown.

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:12.229
Maybe that’s what summoned it.

00:08:12.229 --> 00:08:17.850
Perhaps something had sensed all of Erin’s
mental anguish, and come to seek her out.

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:22.590
It might have been that her wishes for something,
some little alleviation to all the pain and

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:25.010
stress were finally being granted.

00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:29.020
Or maybe it was just a gift left by her neighbor.

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:33.870
The sun had long since set when Erin arrived
back at her apartment, stepping over the envelopes

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:38.819
that littered her doormat; a few with words
like ‘Overdue’ and ‘Urgent Notice’

00:08:38.819 --> 00:08:40.709
printed on them in red ink.

00:08:40.709 --> 00:08:44.639
Passing through the hallway, Erin paused as
she always did, hoping to hear the gentle

00:08:44.639 --> 00:08:47.010
pattering of paws against the floor.

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:51.009
Her therapist had dissuaded her from doing
that, saying it would only make moving on

00:08:51.009 --> 00:08:53.139
from losing Poncho worse.

00:08:53.139 --> 00:08:55.910
She didn’t care, she wanted her dog back.

00:08:55.910 --> 00:09:01.139
And, opening the door to her bedroom, it seemed
like someone had been listening.

00:09:01.139 --> 00:09:04.870
Sat on her bed was a stuffed animal right
in the spot where she’d found her little

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:06.630
friend on that horrible day.

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:12.399
It had been made to look like a dog, specifically
resembling a Jack Russell Terrier, with brown

00:09:12.399 --> 00:09:14.500
patches over its white fur.

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:17.269
The plushie was even wearing a little rain
poncho.

00:09:17.269 --> 00:09:21.829
The sight of it was enough to cause Erin to
break down in tears, weeping in heavy sobs

00:09:21.829 --> 00:09:25.490
as she dashed across the bedroom to hold it
in her arms.

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:30.700
Hugging it tightly, her tears seeping into
the soft fur as she felt it against her face,

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:33.769
she didn’t even think to question where
it had even come from.

00:09:33.769 --> 00:09:37.660
All she wanted to do in that moment was hold
the stuffed animal close.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:43.079
For the first time in what felt like years,
a feeling of relief washed over Erin.

00:09:43.079 --> 00:09:48.680
It was as if everything was melting away – the
stress of work and the toll of her tiring

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:49.889
shifts, gone.

00:09:49.889 --> 00:09:55.350
All the pain from losing Cleo and Poncho dissipated
too, in fact, it felt like she now had her

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:57.110
beloved dog back.

00:09:57.110 --> 00:10:03.560
No, it was better than that, it was almost
as though everything about Poncho, his energy,

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:09.910
his spirit, the way he made her feel so calm
and loved, was all distilled in this stuffed

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:10.910
animal.

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:15.959
And now, it would never grow old, never age
and die, causing her more pain.

00:10:15.959 --> 00:10:20.639
Erin gripped the soft toy tightly; the longer
she held the hug for, the more her stress

00:10:20.639 --> 00:10:22.279
and sadness faded.

00:10:22.279 --> 00:10:27.670
Her face was still wet with tears, although
her sobbing had gradually become low, gentle

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:31.769
chuckles, soon giving way to a peal of uncontrollable
laughter rising in volume.

00:10:31.769 --> 00:10:36.800
It was as though she had taken something and
the very chemistry of her brain was being

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:37.800
altered.

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:43.269
But after so much hardship, it felt good,
to the point where she was almost light-headed.

00:10:43.269 --> 00:10:47.480
Laying down on the floor of her bedroom, arms
locked around the plushie that reminded her

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:52.209
so much of Poncho, Erin continued softly giggling
to herself.

00:10:52.209 --> 00:10:57.639
Her entire body relaxed, so much that every
part of her felt like warm butter, as if she

00:10:57.639 --> 00:11:00.380
was about to start melting through the floorboards.

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:04.699
Although she didn’t know it, or probably
wouldn’t even have cared, her dopamine levels

00:11:04.699 --> 00:11:09.420
were spiking, flooding her body with the bodily
hormone that relieves stress and makes a person

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:10.470
feel good.

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:14.970
In fact, right now, she was feeling better
than she ever had.

00:11:14.970 --> 00:11:20.259
Every day that followed, Erin would come home
to her stuffed animal, her ‘Poncho II’

00:11:20.259 --> 00:11:21.639
as she liked to think of it.

00:11:21.639 --> 00:11:25.569
It didn’t bother her how childlike anyone
else might find it for a grown woman to rely

00:11:25.569 --> 00:11:28.019
so heavily on a plushie for comfort.

00:11:28.019 --> 00:11:31.679
At any rate, it wasn’t something she was
advertising to anyone else.

00:11:31.679 --> 00:11:36.619
After every shift at both of her jobs, she’d
race back to her apartment, right to Poncho

00:11:36.619 --> 00:11:41.089
II, and sit there, just basking in the way
it made her feel.

00:11:41.089 --> 00:11:46.629
It was the most all-encompassing sense of
euphoria and relief, reducing her stress so

00:11:46.629 --> 00:11:51.149
much that she felt like she was floating,
her body lighter than air.

00:11:51.149 --> 00:11:55.680
That feeling was all that mattered to her,
some days she wouldn’t even eat; Poncho

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.279
II was more important to her than food.

00:11:58.279 --> 00:12:01.410
Gradually, she started to become addicted
to that feeling.

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:05.350
Having to wait until the end of her work shifts
to feel that rush of happy chemicals flood

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:07.870
her brain was too long of a delay.

00:12:07.870 --> 00:12:13.209
She started to crave it while working, unable
to focus, feeling erratic and restless without

00:12:13.209 --> 00:12:14.490
Poncho II.

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:18.819
Of course, she couldn’t risk bringing it
into work with her; what if someone took it?

00:12:18.819 --> 00:12:20.110
Or she dropped it?

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:24.630
Her boss might see her with a stuffed animal
at her desk and fire her on the spot, or think

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:27.089
that she was absolutely crazy.

00:12:27.089 --> 00:12:31.070
The only safe place for her source of relief
was at home, but Erin knew that she needed

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:33.959
something to bridge the gap while she was
working.

00:12:33.959 --> 00:12:38.439
The only substitute that worked was taking
a photo of Poncho II on her phone, then blowing

00:12:38.439 --> 00:12:40.489
it up and printing off a copy.

00:12:40.489 --> 00:12:45.290
Erin could carry it around portably, keeping
it in her pocket and taking it out to look

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:47.160
every few minutes while she worked.

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:51.170
The hit of positive chemicals it gave her
wasn’t quite as strong as getting to hold

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:55.790
her stuffed animal; after all, it was just
a grainy photo from her phone, essentially

00:12:55.790 --> 00:13:00.970
acting like a patch to tide her over until
she could get back home to the real thing.

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:05.220
However, it didn’t take long for Erin to
start taking Poncho II with her anywhere that

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:12.300
wasn’t work; to the grocery store, to visit
her mom and her dog, and of course to therapy.

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:17.269
“I’m rather concerned about this pattern
of behavior,” Erin’s psychotherapist,

00:13:17.269 --> 00:13:20.819
Doctor Lee, stated when her patient explained
what had been happening.

00:13:20.819 --> 00:13:25.230
Sitting across on the opposite side of her
office on a leather couch, Erin had Poncho

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:26.889
II pressed tightly against her.

00:13:26.889 --> 00:13:31.540
“I don’t care,” she sighed, her brain
already awash with hormones that kept her

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:32.540
calm.

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:34.980
“I like how it makes me feel, so I don’t
care.”

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:37.389
“Erin, look at yourself,” Doctor Lee urged.

00:13:37.389 --> 00:13:39.980
“You aren’t properly dealing with your
grief.

00:13:39.980 --> 00:13:44.430
It seems to me you’re channeling all your
desire for positive emotion into this stuffed

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:45.430
animal.”

00:13:45.430 --> 00:13:46.430
“Poncho II.”

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:50.290
Erin corrected her without taking her eyes
off the soft brown and white ‘dog’ and

00:13:50.290 --> 00:13:51.290
its little cloak.

00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:56.130
“Listen to me,” the therapist insisted,
trying desperately to get through to her.

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:57.800
“Poncho, your dog…

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:01.959
your real dog is gone, you lost him six months
ago.

00:14:01.959 --> 00:14:04.970
And your mom, Cleo, she passed away too.

00:14:04.970 --> 00:14:10.009
You have to process and come to terms with
those things, as sad as they might make you

00:14:10.009 --> 00:14:11.009
feel.

00:14:11.009 --> 00:14:12.839
That’s how we move on.

00:14:12.839 --> 00:14:15.610
But what you’re doing right now isn’t
healthy, Erin.

00:14:15.610 --> 00:14:17.509
It’s becoming an obsession.”

00:14:17.509 --> 00:14:20.870
Turning away, Erin pressed Poncho II up against
her face.

00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:23.209
“I don’t care,” she repeated.

00:14:23.209 --> 00:14:28.350
By now, Erin had become fully dependent on
Poncho II, showing up late for both jobs just

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:32.960
so she could spend longer feeling those endorphins
and hormones that hugging the stuffed animal

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:34.220
seemed to bring.

00:14:34.220 --> 00:14:39.439
It didn’t take long for her to start skipping
entire shifts for days at a time, and canceling

00:14:39.439 --> 00:14:44.570
any and all other plans just to sit at home
basking in the relief brought on by her apparent

00:14:44.570 --> 00:14:45.570
obsession.

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:50.689
Her apartment became a mess, untidied, piles
of mail by the front door, the walls plastered

00:14:50.689 --> 00:14:55.299
with hundreds of photos of her stuffed animal,
fuelling her obsession.

00:14:55.299 --> 00:14:59.779
Her evening job was the first to fire Erin,
citing her recent absences as the grounds

00:14:59.779 --> 00:15:01.220
for her dismissal.

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:04.129
Even then, she still didn’t seem to care.

00:15:04.129 --> 00:15:07.470
The fact she might not be able to make rent
barely registered.

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:11.279
Returning home after her other job called
her in to tell Erin she would no longer be

00:15:11.279 --> 00:15:15.440
working there either, she instantly looked
around for Poncho II.

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:19.869
But it was nowhere to be found, all the photos
on the walls having faded.

00:15:19.869 --> 00:15:24.829
Erin checked her phone; all the original copies
of the pictures were gone too.

00:15:24.829 --> 00:15:28.240
Instantly wracked with fear, so addicted to
the plushie that she could barely function

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:32.170
without it, she began tearing her apartment
to shreds looking for it.

00:15:32.170 --> 00:15:35.910
She wrenched cupboards off their hinges, and
tipped over her refrigerator.

00:15:35.910 --> 00:15:40.101
Flipping her mattress, Erin sliced the fabric
open with a kitchen knife, searching high

00:15:40.101 --> 00:15:44.929
and low for Poncho II, but unable to find
it anywhere.

00:15:44.929 --> 00:15:50.009
She became frantic, erratic, pulling her hair
out in a fit of uncontrollable despair.

00:15:50.009 --> 00:15:51.199
Where had it gone?

00:15:51.199 --> 00:15:52.470
Had someone stolen it?

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:53.609
Doctor Lee.

00:15:53.609 --> 00:15:58.250
The paranoia had already set in, convincing
Erin that her therapist must have taken Poncho

00:15:58.250 --> 00:15:59.250
II.

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:04.100
She was the only other person who knew about
it and had been so critical of her using it

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:05.889
to make herself feel better.

00:16:05.889 --> 00:16:10.299
Marching into Doctor Lee’s office, utterly
enraged beyond reason, all Erin could think

00:16:10.299 --> 00:16:14.480
about was getting her stuffed animal back,
no matter what she had to do.

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:20.790
The effect it had on her was so powerful,
so potent, and addictive that living without

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:22.929
it was worth anything…

00:16:22.929 --> 00:16:25.949
even another person’s life.

00:16:25.949 --> 00:16:30.430
Little did Erin realize, as her hands grew
wetter, coated with more of Doctor Lee’s

00:16:30.430 --> 00:16:36.049
blood after every bludgeoning strike, her
therapist had no idea where Poncho II was.

00:16:36.049 --> 00:16:40.269
In fact, she had nothing to do with it vanishing
in the first place.

00:16:40.269 --> 00:16:46.170
It had disappeared all on its own, along with
all the photograph copies Erin had printed.

00:16:46.170 --> 00:16:51.850
Arriving somewhere miles away, SCP-589 was
ready to begin the whole cycle again on its

00:16:51.850 --> 00:16:52.850
next victim.

00:16:52.850 --> 00:16:57.410
It would take on whatever shape it needed
to appease the desires of the very next person

00:16:57.410 --> 00:17:03.089
to find it, preying on their vulnerability
and making them totally dependent on it.

00:17:03.089 --> 00:17:08.490
That was what it did, everywhere it went,
leeching off people that it could easily manipulate.

00:17:08.490 --> 00:17:14.339
Its presence and interaction with it would
calm SCP-589’s victims, helping to alleviate

00:17:14.339 --> 00:17:17.860
their stress or make them feel better about
their deepest insecurities.

00:17:17.860 --> 00:17:23.010
Before long, these helpless victims would
be able to think of nothing else, feeling

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:26.299
as if they were unable to live without their
‘Obsession Doll’.

00:17:26.299 --> 00:17:32.190
And, every time, that was when SCP-589 would
make its cruelest move.

00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:35.591
It would vanish, leaving its prey in a state
of intense withdrawal.

00:17:35.591 --> 00:17:41.750
With the calming influence of SCP-589 absent
from their lives all of a sudden, the infected

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:45.860
people would suffer from a variety of potential
psychological symptoms.

00:17:45.860 --> 00:17:52.090
Manic depression, psychosis, uncontrollable
despair, dementia – or, in Erin’s case,

00:17:52.090 --> 00:17:57.310
paranoia and a heightened sense of aggression
that caused her to murder Doctor Lee.

00:17:57.310 --> 00:18:01.590
That was one of the earliest in a spate of
similar incidents that began being reported.

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:07.710
As SCP-589 traveled from town to town, its
influence spread, leaving entire populations

00:18:07.710 --> 00:18:09.350
dead in its wake.

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:14.919
All the while, the stuffed animal fed on the
mental anguish that it caused its victims,

00:18:14.919 --> 00:18:18.460
making them pay the ultimate price for their
obsession.

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:25.799
Now go and check out SCP-2295 The Bear With
a Heart of Patchwork and SCP-6330 Guardians

00:18:25.799 --> 00:18:30.549
of the Innocent for more stuffed animal-related
anomalies contained by the SCP Foundation.

